sometimes you'll see people say this is  backed by science or this is proven by  studies and you'll see a list of  citations but this doesn't mean much  there are a lot of bad studies out there  and it can be really tricky to work out  if a study actually backs up what  someone is saying a lot of the time  abstracts and conclusions will  exaggerate what a study found for  example here's a study that color  science did on how well their products  protected against blue light you would  think that zinc oxide is great for blue  light protection and this is what a lot  of brands and magazine articles and  experts dermatologists estheticians are  saying probably based on this study but  if you actually look at the study they  didn't compare products with and without  zinc oxide so they had zero evidence for  this conclusion and with the products  they did test the product with the least  zinc oxide gave the most blue light  protection which is the opposite of what  they're implying other better design  studies have found that zinc oxide  doesn't give good protection against  blue light even if you have a really  thick white layer so be really careful  when reading studies don't just trust  what they
 

